<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      plMail talks to a server the user owns: a NAS, a home box, a Tailscale
      node. INTERNET is the whole product. ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE lets sync back
      off when there is nothing to reach rather than retrying into a flat
      battery.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      Notifications are asked for at the moment push is switched on, not at
      launch: a permission prompt before the user has connected a server is one
      they have no way to evaluate.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Package visibility for UnifiedPush.

      Since Android 11 an app cannot see another unless it declares a query for
      it. Distributor discovery works by resolving this action, so without this
      block the connector finds no distributors at all on a device where one is
      installed, and push simply never starts, with nothing logged and nothing
      to see. It is the single easiest way for this to look broken.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="org.unifiedpush.android.distributor.REGISTER" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <!--
      android:localeConfig is what puts plMail in Android's own per-app language
      list, from API 33. Without it the system offers no language entry for this
      app at all, and the picker on the Appearance screen would be the only place
      the choice existed — which is the half of the problem that looks like it
      works. The two must be the same setting or a user who sets one finds the
      other disagreeing; see AppLanguages for how they are kept to one answer,
      and res/xml/locales_config.xml for why the list is written by hand.

      UnusedAttribute is ignored because it is right and says nothing useful:
      minSdk is 31 and the attribute does nothing on 31 or 32. That is the whole
      reason AppLocaleOverride exists, and moving the attribute into a
      values-v33 manifest would split one declaration across two files to
      silence a check that is telling us what we already wrote a class about.
    -->
    <application
        android:name=".PlMailApplication"
        tools:ignore="UnusedAttribute"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config"
        android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.PlMail">

        <!--
          No android:label here: it would repeat the application's, and a
          duplicate is one more place to forget when the name changes.
        -->
        <!--
          **No MAIN/LAUNCHER filter here, and its absence is the feature.**

          The launcher entry is carried by the thirty-two LogoLauncher aliases
          below, one per logo colourway, exactly one of them enabled. It cannot
          also be carried here: an always-enabled launcher activity beside an
          enabled alias is two icons on the home screen, and the alternative —
          letting this activity be the default colourway's entry and disabling
          it when the user picks another — means switching the icon disables the
          component this very process is running in. That is the one thing a
          feature which edits somebody's home screen must not do.

          So this activity is never disabled by anything, and the icon is
          switched by turning aliases on and off in front of it. See
          AppLauncherIcon for the switching, and the alias block below for what
          an alias can and cannot override.

          The cost, stated where whoever hits it will look: an install upgrading
          from a build where this activity *did* carry the filter has a home
          screen shortcut resolved to `.MainActivity`, and that component stops
          being a launcher entry. Launchers re-read their entries on a package
          change and put the alias in the drawer, but a shortcut pinned to the
          old component may not survive the swap. It is a one-time cost at one
          upgrade, and there is no shape of this feature that avoids it.
        -->
        <!--
          exported stays true, and not for the launcher: the plmail:// pairing
          link below is the reason. A VIEW filter another app resolves needs it
          as much as a MAIN one does.
        -->
        <!--
          singleTask so a tapped pairing link reaches the activity that is
          already running rather than stacking a second copy of it. Onboarding
          is usually already open when the link is tapped — the user went
          looking for the code because the app asked for one — and a second
          instance would redeem against a ViewModel the visible screen is not
          observing.
        -->
        <!--
          adjustResize, and it is a bug fix rather than tidiness.

          Left unset the mode is SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_UNSPECIFIED, which asks the
          window manager to guess. It guesses by looking for a scrolling view in
          the hierarchy, finds none — a Compose activity is one ComposeView with
          no ScrollView anywhere the platform can recognise — and settles on
          PAN. Panning translates the whole window upward to keep the cursor
          visible, so opening the keyboard in the composer's message body slid
          the top app bar up underneath the status bar, title and back arrow cut
          in half. Nothing in the Compose code could have corrected that: the
          window itself had moved.

          With adjustResize the window stays where it is and the IME arrives as
          an inset instead, which is what the app already knows how to spend —
          see ComposeScreen's imePadding. The two have to be set together:
          adjustResize alone under edge-to-edge would leave the body behind the
          keys, and imePadding alone cannot un-pan a window.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.PlMail"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!--
              The pairing deep link. When the QR is on the same device as the
              app, tapping it skips the camera entirely, which is the whole
              flow — and it is also the fallback when camera permission is
              refused. No autoVerify: plmail:// is our own scheme, not an
              https:// one that needs Digital Asset Links.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="plmail" android:host="pair" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              mailto:, which a mail app not answering is a strange thing.

              Until this filter existed, tapping an email address anywhere on
              this device — a link on a web page, an address in somebody's
              signature, the mail button on a contact card — offered every mail
              client installed except this one. Nothing about it was reported as
              broken because there is nothing to see: the chooser simply does not
              list plMail, and a user concludes the address is not clickable.

              TWO FILTERS FOR TWO DIFFERENT CALLERS, and they are not a
              duplicate of each other.

              VIEW is a link being followed. It carries BROWSABLE, which is what
              a browser requires before it will hand a URI to an app at all: a
              VIEW filter without it is invisible to Chrome, and that is the case
              this exists for.

              SENDTO is an app deliberately asking for a composer on a known
              address — a contacts entry, a "contact us" button, anything built
              on ShareCompat.IntentBuilder. It gets no BROWSABLE, because
              BROWSABLE means "safe to start from untrusted web content" and
              SENDTO is never started that way. Declaring it would be a claim
              about this component that is not true of this action.

              No android:host and no path. A mailto: is an opaque URI: everything
              after the colon is the scheme-specific part, so there is no
              authority for a host to match and a host attribute here would match
              nothing at all. See Mailto for what is done with the rest of it.

              Both land on MainActivity, which is singleTask, so a mailto:
              tapped while plMail is open reaches the running instance through
              onNewIntent rather than stacking a second copy over the mailbox.
              MainActivity already had to sort a pairing link out of its intent;
              it now sorts three kinds, and by scheme, because both this and the
              pairing filter are VIEW and the scheme is the only thing that tells
              them apart once the intent is in hand.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
                <data android:scheme="mailto" />
            </intent-filter>

            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SENDTO" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:scheme="mailto" />
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              The share sheet.

              **`*/*`, and it is a product decision rather than a shortcut.**

              The alternative is an enumeration — text/plain, image/*, video/*,
              audio/*, application/pdf — and the enumeration is wrong the first
              time somebody shares a .zip, a .docx, an .apk, a .gpx or a
              calendar invitation. A mail message can carry any of those; there
              is no type a mail client is genuinely unable to attach, so any
              list here is a list of the files a user is told, with no
              explanation, that they may not email. "Why can't I send this from
              plMail" is a worse bug than the one `*/*` buys, and it is a bug
              with no error message attached to it.

              What `*/*` costs, stated plainly because it is real: plMail now
              appears in the share sheet of everything on the device, including
              sheets where mailing the thing makes no sense. That is the same
              bargain every mail client on the platform makes — Gmail and K-9
              both claim `*/*` — and the sheet sorts by use, so an entry nobody
              picks sinks. It is a row of noise against a category of files that
              silently cannot be sent.

              A note on what `*/*` does NOT match: an intent with no type at all.
              That is a malformed share and there is nothing useful to do with
              one, so it is left to fall through to whoever else wants it.

              SEND and SEND_MULTIPLE in one filter, because the difference is
              only whether EXTRA_STREAM holds a Uri or a list of them and
              ShareIntake reads both. Splitting them would mean two filters that
              must not drift.

              text/plain needs no separate mention: `*/*` matches it, and it is
              the one branch that produces a *body* rather than an attachment.
              Where that happens is ShareIntake, not here.

              THE COST OF singleTask, which belongs on the record. A share into
              an already-running plMail brings the mail task forward rather than
              starting a task on top of the sharing app, so backing out of the
              composer reveals the inbox and not the gallery the photo came
              from. The alternative is a second activity with its own
              taskAffinity, the way CalendarActivity has one — but the composer
              is hoisted in MainActivity together with the undo-send host that
              outlives it, and a second copy over there would be a second undo
              window that the first one does not know about. One task and one
              composer was the cheaper of the two wrong things.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
                <data android:mimeType="*/*" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!--
          The calendar as its own app, off unless the user asks for it.

          THE TASK MODEL, which is the whole reason this is an activity of its
          own rather than an extra door into MainActivity.

          What was wanted: a second launcher icon that forms a SEPARATE task, so
          the calendar gets its own card in Recents, its own back stack, and can
          sit beside the mail in split screen. Back out of the calendar app
          leaves it, rather than revealing an inbox the user never opened.

          What an activity-alias can and cannot do. Its attribute set is a
          subset of an activity's: name, targetActivity, label, icon, roundIcon,
          permission, exported, enabled. Everything outside that subset is
          inherited from the target and CANNOT be overridden on the alias, and
          taskAffinity and launchMode are both outside it. An alias pointed at
          MainActivity therefore inherits MainActivity's affinity and its
          singleTask, which means the launcher icon would reach the mail task
          that is already running and hand it an onNewIntent. One card in
          Recents, one back stack, no split screen: exactly what was not wanted,
          and it would have looked like it worked on the first tap.

          So the affinity is declared where it is legal, on a second activity,
          and the alias inherits it from there. That inheritance is the
          mechanism rather than a workaround: the alias exists to carry the
          icon, the label and the enabled flag the settings toggle writes, and
          the activity behind it exists to own the task.

          taskAffinity=":calendar" and not a spelled-out name. A leading colon
          is expanded at install time against the applicationId, so this is
          "de.plmail:calendar" in the foss build and "de.plmail.google:calendar"
          in the other, with ".debug" folded in for a debug build. Two flavours
          installed side by side, which this project does while it compares the
          push paths, therefore cannot end up sharing a calendar task.

          launchMode="singleTask" for the same reason MainActivity has it, and
          with the same effect confined to this affinity: tapping the calendar
          icon while the calendar is already open brings that task forward
          rather than stacking a second calendar. The two activities never
          contend for one instance, because there is no one instance to contend
          for. That is the part an alias over MainActivity could not have given.

          exported="false" on the activity is correct and is not the mistake it
          would look like. The launcher resolves the ALIAS, and it is the
          alias's exported flag that the permission check reads; the target is
          then instantiated inside our own process. Nothing outside the app can
          name this activity directly.

          enabled="false" on the alias is the default state, and it is the fact
          the settings toggle flips through PackageManager. It is deliberately
          not mirrored into DataStore: the system owns this, and a second copy
          would be a second copy that can be wrong.
        -->
        <!--
          adjustResize here too, for the reason MainActivity's note gives. The
          calendar app has an event editor with text fields in it, so it is the
          same window that would otherwise be panned — and two activities onto
          the same install disagreeing about how the keyboard arrives is the
          kind of difference nobody notices until one of them is reported.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".CalendarActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:taskAffinity=":calendar"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.PlMail"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!--
          Its own label and its own icon, both in resources so German gets the
          word it actually uses. Declared here in src/main rather than per
          flavour, so foss and google both have it without either being able to
          drift from the other.
        -->
        <activity-alias
            android:name=".CalendarLauncher"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:exported="true"
            android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_calendar"
            android:label="@string/calendar_app_name"
            android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_calendar_round"
            android:targetActivity=".CalendarActivity">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!--
          THE LAUNCHER ICON, one alias per logo colourway.

          The user picks a colourway on the web — thirty-two of them, `LogoStyle`
          on the server, default `berry` — and the JMAP `Appearance` singleton
          publishes the choice as a read-only `logoStyle` string. The phone's
          launcher icon follows it, and an alias per colourway is the only way an
          Android app can have an icon that changes: `android:icon` on a
          component is a resource reference resolved by the *launcher*, out of
          this manifest, long before any of our code runs. There is no API that
          hands a launcher a bitmap for an installed app, so what can be changed
          is not the icon but which component is showing one.

          What an alias can and cannot do is set out at length on CalendarLauncher
          above and applies here unchanged: name, targetActivity, label, icon,
          roundIcon, permission, exported and enabled are the whole of an alias's
          attribute set, and everything else — taskAffinity, launchMode — is
          inherited from the target and is not settable here. That inheritance is
          wanted this time rather than worked around. Every one of these points at
          MainActivity, so every one of them inherits its default affinity and its
          singleTask, and all thirty-two are therefore doors into the same one
          mail task. Which door the user came through changes the icon they
          tapped and nothing else about the app, which is exactly the promise.

          **Exactly one is enabled at any moment, and never zero.** `berry` is
          enabled here, in the manifest, so a fresh install has a launcher entry
          before the app has ever run and an older server that publishes no
          logoStyle leaves it alone. AppLauncherIcon enables the new alias
          *before* disabling the old one, so there is no instant — not one — in
          which this app has no launcher entry. An app that vanishes from a home
          screen for a hundred milliseconds is an app some launchers will not put
          back.

          label is inherited on purpose, unlike CalendarLauncher's. These are all
          the mail app; a label here would be @string/app_name written thirty-two
          more times, and thirty-two more places to forget it.

          icon and roundIcon are pointed at the same drawable. See the header of
          any generated mipmap for why: roundIcon predates adaptive icons and
          means nothing at minSdk 31, but left undeclared it would inherit the
          application's — which is the *default* colourway, so a launcher asking
          for the round icon would get berry whatever the user had chosen.

          Generated, all thirty-two of them, by tools/generate_launcher_icons.py
          from tools/logostyles.json — which was extracted by running the
          server's own enum. Edit the table and rerun; do not hand-edit between
          the markers. The same script writes LogoStyle.kt, so the names the app
          switches at runtime and the names declared here come from one source.
        -->
        <!-- BEGIN generated launcher aliases: tools/generate_launcher_icons.py -->
        <activity-alias
